    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper experimentally tests whether gradient-norm penalization and layer selection improve validation accuracy, but it does not test the listed claim versions directly.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports validation accuracy, gradient norms, and throughput for gradient-penalizing methods. It does not measure training-to-test error gaps, random-label fitting, the listed architecture or optimizer comparisons, dropout, magnitude-aware sharpness, or removal of memorized examples.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses the broader premise that flatter solutions improve generalization to develop an efficient SAM variant, reporting its accuracy and computational performance. It tests none of V1–V8. In particular, gradient norms and layer-wise loss landscapes are not V7's magnitude-aware perturbation-tolerance measure or its comparison against generalization gaps.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is substantially accurate: the authors conclude that perturbing selected critical layers suppresses whole-model gradient norms and improves validation performance while reducing SAM's computational overhead. They also claim a SAM-like convergence rate under their assumptions. Table 1 shows comparable accuracy to generalized SAM, with slightly lower accuracy for ViT fine-tuning rather than uniformly higher accuracy.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study proposes layer-wise gradient-norm penalization and compares it with mini-batch SGD and SAM variants. Its image-classification experiments use standard datasets; no random-label memorization protocol is used.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Validation accuracy and throughput vary with dataset, model, layer-selection criterion, and number of perturbed layers k. The paper reports low-norm selection below high-norm and random selection in the tested benchmarks, and throughput declines as k increases.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Selective high-gradient layer perturbation improves validation accuracy over SGD in the four Table 1 settings and gives accuracy close to full-model generalized SAM. Layer-selection and perturbation-count ablations show that accuracy depends on which layers and how many layers are perturbed.",
        "proposed_claim": "Perturbing a small set of high-gradient layers can retain SAM-like validation accuracy with less computation than full-model perturbation.",
